WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.800
Cool, all right.

00:06.800 --> 00:12.680
So today we're going to continue our discussion about localization.

00:12.680 --> 00:16.820
And then we will move to the topic of simultaneous localization and mapping.

00:16.820 --> 00:21.720
In particular, if you remember, a key assumption we made last time in order to solve the localization

00:21.720 --> 00:29.400
problem was to assume that we know the correspondence variables, which means that an oracle tells

00:29.400 --> 00:37.180
us every time we have a measurement to which landmark that measurement corresponds to.

00:37.180 --> 00:42.240
And this was captured, if you remember, through the notion of a correspondence variable.

00:42.240 --> 00:51.920
In particular, we say that CTi is equal to j if measurement i taken at time t corresponds

00:51.920 --> 00:59.760
to landmark j, say to the point j in a map that for now we assume is given to us.

00:59.760 --> 01:06.400
The natural question, though, is who gives us these correspondence variables?

01:06.400 --> 01:13.960
In general, indeed, they're not known and need to be estimated as we estimate our pose

01:13.960 --> 01:16.920
or we need a given map.

01:16.920 --> 01:24.600
The way to do it is to determine the identity of a landmark during localization via maximum

01:24.600 --> 01:30.560
likelihood estimation, whereby one first determines the most likely value of the correspondence

01:30.560 --> 01:33.560
variable CT, and then it takes it for granted.

01:33.560 --> 01:36.440
Now, these are some ramifications.

01:36.440 --> 01:43.040
First of all, the idea is to estimate, so to take the value for the correspondence variable

01:43.040 --> 01:46.400
that best explain the data that we have observed.

01:46.400 --> 01:47.520
That's reasonable.

01:47.520 --> 01:54.920
But then we take this value for granted, which means that if we make a mistake in the correspondence,

01:54.920 --> 01:57.800
we're fully committed to that choice.

01:57.800 --> 02:03.240
And so we carry this mistake over and over in subsequent time steps.

02:03.240 --> 02:10.720
So using maximum likelihood is generally a good idea because it leads, as we will see,

02:10.720 --> 02:15.440
to a fairly tractable algorithm to estimate the correspondence variables, but introduces

02:15.480 --> 02:21.120
some brittleness in the localization algorithm due to the fact that we might commit ourselves

02:21.120 --> 02:28.560
to correspondences that are erroneous, which means that you need to add, in general, some

02:28.560 --> 02:33.960
additional levels of robustness, for example, in terms of outlier rejection schemes.

02:33.960 --> 02:39.080
Or simply, you track many different landmarks so that even if you have a few mistakes here

02:39.080 --> 02:43.560
and there, it's not catastrophic during your localization process.

02:43.960 --> 02:56.520
Having said that, the idea, as I said, to estimate the correspondence variables is to

02:56.520 --> 03:08.920
pick the assignments from measurements to landmarks that makes the measurements as likely

03:08.920 --> 03:09.920
as possible.

03:10.520 --> 03:13.440
M is the map that we assume is given.

03:13.440 --> 03:15.960
UT is the history of controls.

03:15.960 --> 03:20.320
Z1 to T minus 1 is the history of measurements.

03:20.320 --> 03:27.000
And C1 to T is the history of assignments for the correspondence variable.

03:27.000 --> 03:36.360
The correspondence variable from time step 1 to time step T minus 1 have been determined

03:36.360 --> 03:40.200
in the previous iteration of the estimation procedure.

03:40.200 --> 03:45.120
What we are trying to understand is how to select the assignment at time step T.

03:45.120 --> 03:52.640
Is there a different algorithm that uses the popular distribution for C1 to T?

03:52.640 --> 03:55.200
Yeah, so this is the most basic one.

03:55.200 --> 04:02.480
For example, a more advanced version is a multi-apothesis algorithm whereby you keep

04:02.480 --> 04:10.160
multiple hypotheses about your localization, each one corresponding to a given history

04:10.160 --> 04:11.560
of the correspondence variables.

04:11.560 --> 04:13.440
And then they are mixed probabilistically.

04:13.440 --> 04:19.040
With this one, if you mess up one of the Cs, you might be pretty bad off going forward.

04:19.040 --> 04:23.760
Well, you keep this error as time moves forward.

04:23.760 --> 04:26.880
That's exactly what I was saying before.

04:26.880 --> 04:31.520
Now, having said that, in general you have many landmarks.

04:31.520 --> 04:37.120
So even if you screw up with a landmark, since you are also observing many other landmarks,

04:37.120 --> 04:40.200
the localization process still can be reasonable.

04:40.200 --> 04:45.480
But second of all, you can add additional layers of robustness to this basic scheme

04:45.480 --> 04:51.320
by either reason about multiple hypotheses for this correspondence variable, so have

04:51.320 --> 04:57.040
basically a mixture localization algorithm or some other rejection and so on.

04:57.040 --> 05:01.440
I'm just going to provide references for those methodologies, but your point is very valid.

05:02.160 --> 05:10.920
Anyway, so the point here is that we say, OK, if Ct is equal to some assignment, what

05:10.920 --> 05:13.760
is the probability of the measurement that I just observed?

05:13.760 --> 05:19.360
I do that for all possible assignments of measurement i to lemma j, and I pick the assignment

05:19.360 --> 05:23.560
that maximizes the likelihood of the measurement that I have observed.

05:23.560 --> 05:25.360
So it's fairly reasonable.

05:25.360 --> 05:31.160
I consider all possible assignments, I pick the one that best explains my current measurement

05:31.640 --> 05:35.680
as captured by the condition distribution of the measurement with respect to previous

05:35.680 --> 05:38.960
assignment, previous measurement, and previous controls.

05:38.960 --> 05:43.560
So the intuition should be fairly straightforward.

05:43.560 --> 05:52.640
Now, the challenge is that there are exponentially many assignments.

05:52.640 --> 06:00.520
So Ct, so remember that the Ct, the measurement, indeed entails many possible measurements

06:00.520 --> 06:01.520
at time t.

06:01.520 --> 06:05.160
For example, if you have a lighter sensors, you have a point cloud.

06:05.160 --> 06:14.480
So Zt is indeed a vector with many components, including Zti.

06:14.480 --> 06:17.960
And we want to find an assignment for each one of these components to a landmark.

06:17.960 --> 06:23.560
And the number of possibilities is exponential in the number of measurements, which in general

06:23.560 --> 06:31.880
makes the direct computation of the maximum likelihood extremely computationally intense.

06:31.880 --> 06:42.440
So a typical approach is to use an approximation and indeed perform maximum likelihood estimation

06:42.440 --> 06:48.200
separately per each measurement.

06:48.200 --> 06:57.440
So instead of processing all measurements Zt in batch, I process each assignment one

06:57.440 --> 06:59.040
by one.

06:59.040 --> 07:03.880
So we look at actually maximum likelihood with respect to the specific measurement I

07:03.880 --> 07:07.960
as opposed to the entire vector measurements.

07:07.960 --> 07:12.880
Any questions about this?

07:12.880 --> 07:19.280
And now the optimization is no longer in terms of Ct, but becomes an optimization in terms

07:19.280 --> 07:22.640
of a Cti.

07:22.640 --> 07:28.360
So for each measurement, Cti, I try to find the correspondence from measurement at Zti

07:28.360 --> 07:36.000
to landmark j through the correspondence variable Cti, such that the likelihood of that measurement

07:36.000 --> 07:41.120
conditional on the histogram of measurement controls and conditional on a specific value

07:41.120 --> 07:45.680
of the assignment for Cti is maximized.

07:45.680 --> 07:48.280
Is this measurement related to landmark 1?

07:48.280 --> 07:51.840
If that is the case, what would be the probability of observing a measurement that I've just

07:51.840 --> 07:52.840
observed?

07:52.840 --> 07:53.840
0.3.

07:53.840 --> 07:57.540
Is this measurement related to landmark 2?

07:57.540 --> 08:01.640
If that is the case, what is the probability of observing a measurement that I've just

08:01.640 --> 08:02.640
observed?

08:02.640 --> 08:03.640
0.5.

08:03.640 --> 08:07.120
OK, so it's better to associate this measurement with landmark 2.

08:07.120 --> 08:08.460
That's what we're saying.

08:08.460 --> 08:17.300
So the only additional challenge is to represent this probability distribution in an analytical

08:17.300 --> 08:21.120
form so that actually we can solve this optimization problem.

08:21.120 --> 08:26.860
To do so in the EKF spirit, what we do is to use linearizations.

08:26.860 --> 08:33.380
The mathematics as usual tend to be tedious, not complicated, but tedious.

08:33.380 --> 08:37.940
