25:46.440 --> 25:50.400
covariance matrix.

25:50.400 --> 25:56.160
Which means that, loosely speaking, if on some axis you are very uncertain, then the

25:56.160 --> 26:00.720
sigma is very large, and then when you divide the Euclidean distance by the large sigma,

26:00.720 --> 26:04.440
you get a small number, which means that statistically you're indeed quite close.

26:04.440 --> 26:11.640
This is an explanation that from a mathematical standpoint is extremely brutal and inanimate,

26:11.640 --> 26:12.640
but I think is very practical.

26:12.640 --> 26:13.640
Do you agree?

26:13.840 --> 26:15.840
Brutal, but effective.

26:18.840 --> 26:26.600
Okay, so the idea then of using melanobis distance to do that association is, again,

26:26.600 --> 26:35.040
you get a measurement, you consider all possible associations for the correspondence variable.

26:35.040 --> 26:42.560
For each one of them, you compute an expected measurement that you should get if measurement

26:42.600 --> 26:55.720
i corresponds to landmark k with associated uncertainty, and so on.

26:55.720 --> 27:02.800
Then you compute the melanobis distance of the measurement that you have observed from

27:02.800 --> 27:10.680
each one of the expected measurements that you should have gotten with respect to a given

27:10.680 --> 27:15.080
correspondence variable association, and you pick the one that minimizes the melanobis

27:15.080 --> 27:16.080
distance.

27:16.080 --> 27:21.160
This is yet another way of basically picking a value for the correspondence variable that

27:21.160 --> 27:23.160
best explains the data.

27:30.160 --> 27:34.160
We'll see this better later on when we talk about EK epsilon.

27:38.160 --> 27:39.160
Does this make sense?

27:39.640 --> 27:43.640
Again, the idea is to get a correspondence variable that best explains the data.

27:43.640 --> 27:48.640
You can do it either through maximum likelihood estimation or by minimizing the notion of

27:48.640 --> 27:49.640
melanobis distance.

27:58.640 --> 28:01.640
So usually, it can be a sign of one or two things.

28:01.640 --> 28:04.640
Everybody understands or everybody doesn't understand.

28:07.640 --> 28:08.640
Should I keep going?

28:09.640 --> 28:10.640
Yeah.

28:11.640 --> 28:17.640
Is this notion of melanobis distance kind of also like maximum likelihood estimation

28:17.640 --> 28:18.640
because you're taking the point?

28:18.640 --> 28:19.640
Yes.

28:19.640 --> 28:20.640
That makes sense.

28:20.640 --> 28:22.640
In a way, it's very much related.

28:22.640 --> 28:24.640
The philosophy is the same.

28:24.640 --> 28:32.640
We want to consider the association that best explains what we have observed.

28:32.640 --> 28:35.640
But what does it mean best explains what we have observed?

28:36.120 --> 28:41.120
In this case, best explained means that the distance between what we have observed and

28:41.120 --> 28:50.120
what we think we should have observed if measurement i corresponding to ln j, this distance is

28:50.120 --> 28:51.120
as small as possible.

28:51.120 --> 28:57.120
But small as through the lens of melanobis distance, not Euclidean distance, such that

28:57.120 --> 29:01.120
we have a probabilistic interpretation of the distance in terms of sigmas away from

29:01.120 --> 29:04.120
the mean as opposed to just a geometric interpretation of the distance.

29:06.120 --> 29:07.120
Cool.

29:07.120 --> 29:13.120
So as mentioned before, this approach is powerful, but it's quite brittle.

29:13.120 --> 29:18.120
Because if you make erroneous associations, then you keep those through time.

29:18.120 --> 29:20.120
So there are different ways to deal with it.

29:20.120 --> 29:24.120
And this is a little bit beyond the scope of the class.

29:24.120 --> 29:29.120
But one way that actually was suggested by one of your colleagues is to consider a multi-apothesis

29:29.120 --> 29:34.120
stacking filter where there is a belief that is represented as a mixture of Gaussians.

29:34.600 --> 29:39.600
And each Gaussian tracks a different sequence of association decisions.

29:41.600 --> 29:44.600
And of course, you don't have to use necessarily EKF.

29:44.600 --> 29:49.600
You can also use other parametric features for localizations such as EKF.

29:49.600 --> 29:50.600
That is another popular choice.

29:50.600 --> 29:53.600
But again, this is beyond what we do in this class.

29:54.600 --> 29:58.600
OK, one more item for localization.

29:59.080 --> 30:04.080
Here, those of you that are undergrads or taking 174 should take a break.

30:04.080 --> 30:05.080
Well, a break.

30:05.080 --> 30:08.080
Don't step out, because it's going to be just five minutes.

30:08.080 --> 30:11.080
But you're not responsible for this material.

30:11.080 --> 30:16.080
Those of you that are actually in the 200th version of this class should listen to what I'm saying.

30:16.080 --> 30:24.080
So far, we have talked about parametric approaches to localization, whereby in particular,

30:24.080 --> 30:31.080
we consider a Gaussian as a functional representation of the belief, as an approximation.

30:32.080 --> 30:36.080
But as we saw during the discussion about filtering,

30:36.080 --> 30:41.080
another possible approach is to consider non-parametric approximation.

30:42.080 --> 30:49.080
Whereby, in a way or another, we approximate the belief through a set of particles.

30:49.080 --> 30:55.080
Particles, again, are concrete instantiation of the value of the state.

30:55.080 --> 31:04.080
So we saw last time how this approach led to a fairly powerful filtering algorithm

31:04.080 --> 31:06.080
referred to as a particle filter.

31:07.080 --> 31:16.080
The idea, as it was the case for EKF, is to take that algorithm from filtering theory,

31:16.080 --> 31:19.080
cast it in a framework of robotic localization,

31:19.080 --> 31:23.080
essentially understanding what the state transition model in this case is,

31:23.080 --> 31:26.080
what the measurement model in this case is, and so on and so forth,

31:26.080 --> 31:29.080
and then obtain the resulting localization algorithm.

31:29.080 --> 31:32.080
This is exactly what we're going to do for the particle filter.

31:32.080 --> 31:36.080
In particular, the application of the particle filter to the problem of localization

31:36.080 --> 31:40.080
is referred to as Monte Carlo localization.

31:40.080 --> 31:46.080
The good news is that the application of the filter to the localization problem

31:46.080 --> 31:50.080
in the case of the particle filter is really straightforward.

31:50.080 --> 31:55.080
The EKF was more complicated, but the particle filter approach is extremely simple.

31:55.080 --> 32:00.080
What you do is literally you take the particle filter that we explained last Friday,

32:00.080 --> 32:04.080
you just substitute the state transition model and the measurement model

32:04.080 --> 32:08.080
with the appropriate instantiations of the state transition model

32:08.080 --> 32:12.080
and the measurement models for robotics, and that's it.

32:13.080 --> 32:17.080
In particular, if you remember, the idea of the particle filter

32:17.080 --> 32:21.080
is to represent a belief through a set of particles, capital M,

32:21.080 --> 32:23.080
usually about a thousand.

32:23.080 --> 32:28.080
And then the filter has the same structure of a bias filter,

32:28.080 --> 32:31.080
so we have as an input the belief at time t minus one,

32:31.080 --> 32:35.080
current measurement, current control, and for now assume that we have a map,

32:35.080 --> 32:40.080
we produce the belief at time t, again, representing the set of M particles.

32:40.080 --> 32:43.080
What we do, as usual, is a prediction step

32:43.080 --> 32:48.080
where we propagate forward the particles by using the state transition model

32:48.080 --> 32:52.080
and then we correct the prediction by using information from the measurement.

32:52.080 --> 32:57.080
The way the particle filter does it is to propagate the particle forward

32:57.080 --> 33:00.080
by using the state transition model.

33:01.080 --> 33:08.080
Compute an important weight that measures how likely the measurement

33:08.080 --> 33:15.080
that I have observed is assuming that the state has a value

33:15.080 --> 33:19.080
equal to the value of that particle, again, in the map.

33:21.080 --> 33:23.080
I do this for all particles.

33:23.080 --> 33:29.080
In the second step, what I do is to resample the set of particles

33:29.080 --> 33:34.080
with a placement with the probability that it is proportional with importance weight,

33:34.080 --> 33:38.080
whose notion, as usual, is to capture how compatible the measurement

33:38.080 --> 33:43.080
that I have observed is with the hypothesis that the state

33:43.080 --> 33:46.080
is equal to the value captured by the particle M.

33:46.080 --> 33:48.080
Note that it is sampling with a placement,

33:48.080 --> 33:52.080
so it's possible that as a resample, I always resample the same particle.

33:52.080 --> 33:57.080
In the case where, and this happens when, for whatever reason,

33:57.080 --> 34:02.080
given a given particle, the measurement that I've observed

34:02.080 --> 34:06.080
has probability equal to one, and given all the other particles

34:06.080 --> 34:09.080
the measurements I have observed has probability equal to zero.

34:09.080 --> 34:13.080
So that I have one particle that corresponds to me to look in that direction,

34:13.080 --> 34:16.080
one particle that corresponds to me to look into that direction,

34:16.080 --> 34:19.080
and one particle that corresponds to me to look in that direction.

34:19.080 --> 34:21.080
And what I observe is a table.

34:22.080 --> 34:26.080
So clearly, when I resample, the probability of sampling this particle

34:26.080 --> 34:29.660
particle is 1, the probability of sampling those two particles

34:29.660 --> 34:31.740
is equal to 0, because those two particles are

34:31.740 --> 34:35.060
incompatible with a measurement that we just observed.

34:35.060 --> 34:38.860
And so I will have, in the case where we only

34:38.860 --> 34:41.700
have three particles, the new set will be still

34:41.700 --> 34:43.140
set with three particles, but will

34:43.140 --> 34:44.540
be the same particle repeated.

34:47.700 --> 34:48.660
And that's it.

34:48.660 --> 34:54.300
So in hindsight, this filter is much easier to explain,

34:54.300 --> 34:55.740
much easier to code.

34:55.760 --> 34:58.280
Actually, the third problem in homework four

34:58.280 --> 35:06.160
will ask you to code Monte Carlo localization with a tweak.

35:06.160 --> 35:08.440
I would say I want to introduce a tweak.

35:08.440 --> 35:11.900
The tweak is that here, the particles

35:11.900 --> 35:13.560
are sampled in a way that is completely

35:13.560 --> 35:15.600
independent from each other.

35:15.600 --> 35:18.120
And so sometimes, if you're unlucky,

35:18.120 --> 35:20.760
you might have the issue that you get the same particle over

35:20.760 --> 35:24.560
and over, and that may lead to the filter to diverge.

35:24.560 --> 35:26.420
So there are better ways to resampling

35:26.420 --> 35:28.420
by using a stochastic process.

35:28.420 --> 35:33.700
And that's what you will do for problem three, courtesy of.

35:33.700 --> 35:35.340
But it's actually interesting.

35:35.340 --> 35:36.700
It's fairly simple.

35:36.700 --> 35:40.700
It's an interesting addition to the Monte Carlo localization

35:40.700 --> 35:43.420
algorithm to make it remarkably more robust.

35:43.420 --> 35:43.920
Yeah?

35:43.920 --> 35:46.540
How do we incorporate the map into the measurement

35:46.540 --> 35:48.180
model and the state transition model?

35:48.180 --> 35:50.060
How does that affect our distribution?

35:50.480 --> 35:57.840
So this is something that we discussed last time.

35:57.840 --> 36:00.200
So the way that we incorporate the map in the measurement

36:00.200 --> 36:02.900
model and the state transition model

36:02.900 --> 36:05.360
is exactly the same, regardless of whether we

36:05.360 --> 36:08.400
use the EKF algorithm and the particle filter algorithm.

36:08.400 --> 36:10.700
So for example, for the state transition model,

36:10.700 --> 36:12.560
if you remember from last time, we

36:12.560 --> 36:16.560
had a little bit of discussion about what happened.

36:16.580 --> 36:22.900
So when you forward propagate the dynamics, so you're here.

36:22.900 --> 36:27.160
And this could be the possible realization of your state,

36:27.160 --> 36:30.540
maybe with more mass here, at the next time.

36:30.540 --> 36:32.660
But now with the map, we have the additional knowledge

36:32.660 --> 36:34.660
that we might have obstacles.

36:34.660 --> 36:37.980
So that should reshape this distribution.

36:37.980 --> 36:40.860
And so we said that the way to do so

36:40.860 --> 36:44.500
is to multiply the state transition model in the case

36:44.520 --> 36:52.320
that we have no knowledge of the map per times a term that

36:52.320 --> 36:55.760
checks how compatible a state is with respect to a map.

36:55.760 --> 36:57.080
It's the same business here.

36:57.080 --> 36:58.880
So the discussion we had last time

36:58.880 --> 37:00.760
ports directly to here.

37:00.760 --> 37:05.240
So this is an algorithm that is relatively simple to implement

37:05.240 --> 37:07.160
conceptually.

37:07.160 --> 37:09.120
Quite robust, it addresses global localization,

37:09.120 --> 37:11.080
position tracking, kidnapping of other problems,

37:11.080 --> 37:12.200
so very general.

37:12.200 --> 37:14.000
It can also handle dynamic environments

37:14.000 --> 37:16.540
by doing a trial rejection and so on.

37:16.540 --> 37:17.620
But there are some caveats.

37:17.620 --> 37:25.140
So as you'll see in your Pset, here we're doing a for loop.

37:25.140 --> 37:26.980
So if you're not careful, if you have

37:26.980 --> 37:28.860
a large number of particles, this algorithm

37:28.860 --> 37:30.700
becomes very slow.

37:30.700 --> 37:33.440
So implementation here is very important in order

37:33.440 --> 37:37.140
to have a real-time particle filter.

37:37.140 --> 37:39.420
And that's something that you'll see in the Pset.

37:39.420 --> 37:41.940
Second of all, you might have more advanced versions

37:41.940 --> 37:43.980
of assembling the particles.

37:43.980 --> 37:47.360
In order to add robustness.

37:47.360 --> 37:50.440
Third of all, and this is the example here.

37:50.440 --> 37:52.360
So this is an application of a particle filter.

37:52.360 --> 37:54.960
So we have the robot that is moving

37:54.960 --> 37:58.000
and is getting line of sight measurements.

37:58.000 --> 38:00.000
So the cluster of particles corresponds

38:00.000 --> 38:03.160
to a hypothesis about what the filter thinks the robot is.

38:03.160 --> 38:04.960
In reality, the robot is here.

38:04.960 --> 38:07.620
The most likely hypothesis are that the robot is here

38:07.620 --> 38:08.320
and it is here.

38:08.320 --> 38:10.120
Because based on previous measurements,

38:10.120 --> 38:12.520
this is compatible with both poses.

38:12.520 --> 38:15.940
As we get inside and we start seeing a table that is not

38:15.940 --> 38:21.980
here, these particles disappear when we do the re-sampling.

38:21.980 --> 38:24.260
And finally, all the particles are concentrated

38:24.260 --> 38:26.900
around the right pose.

38:26.900 --> 38:29.740
So this is a nominal operation of a particle filter.

38:29.740 --> 38:33.280
When you start, if you know nothing about your environment,

38:33.280 --> 38:35.260
you just sprinkle the particles everywhere.

38:35.260 --> 38:37.540
Remember, a particle is an hypothesis

38:37.540 --> 38:40.820
about the concrete instantiation for your state value.

38:40.820 --> 38:41.860
You might get unlucky.

38:41.860 --> 38:44.560
And for whatever reason, in a bad round,

38:44.560 --> 38:47.240
you eliminate all particles but one.

38:47.240 --> 38:50.260
So there is one particle that is repeated 1,000 times.

38:50.260 --> 38:52.360
That's a problem of particle starvation.

38:52.360 --> 38:55.120
You don't have enough particles to represent your belief.

38:55.120 --> 38:58.200
So what people do in practice is, from time to time,

38:58.200 --> 39:02.480
to sprinkle some particles here and there,

39:02.480 --> 39:05.360
which corresponds to the notion that your robot might have

39:05.360 --> 39:06.880
been kidnapped.

39:06.880 --> 39:09.880
And so it's good to have some residual density everywhere

39:09.900 --> 39:12.000
in the environment.

39:12.000 --> 39:13.500
Yeah?

39:13.500 --> 39:16.500
Environments that are very similar, but symmetric.

39:16.500 --> 39:19.060
So you have, say, the same room above and the same room

39:19.060 --> 39:19.560
below.

39:19.560 --> 39:21.620
So there's no way to tell.

39:21.620 --> 39:22.380
Exactly.

39:22.380 --> 39:23.980
This is actually, if you have algorithm,

39:23.980 --> 39:24.820
it wouldn't work well.

39:24.820 --> 39:26.280
Because that's really the case where

39:26.280 --> 39:29.180
you need to have two modes in your belief.

39:29.180 --> 39:31.120
If the environment is symmetric and you're just

39:31.120 --> 39:32.980
relying on the measurements, there

39:32.980 --> 39:35.380
is for you no way to tell whether you

39:35.380 --> 39:37.660
are going in that direction and that direction.

39:37.660 --> 39:40.280
So it makes sense that you have a belief distribution that

39:40.280 --> 39:41.620
has two peaks.

39:41.620 --> 39:44.920
And you keep track of both of them simultaneously.

39:44.920 --> 39:46.760
Until, hopefully, you get some measurement

39:46.760 --> 39:49.320
that allows you to disambiguate where you are.

39:49.320 --> 39:52.900
Now, that's exactly what is happening here.

39:52.900 --> 39:55.120
The corner is actually until actually three.

39:55.120 --> 39:55.620
Exactly.

39:55.620 --> 39:58.300
So if you see here, this cluster,

39:58.300 --> 40:04.080
it maps into this cluster here, which is getting into here.

40:04.080 --> 40:05.880
That's exactly what's happening here.

40:05.880 --> 40:07.380
But it's a power of the particle filter

40:07.400 --> 40:09.520
that allows you to keep track of these modes.

40:09.520 --> 40:10.740
With EKF, you can't.

40:10.740 --> 40:14.280
You have a big Gaussian that smooths everything.

40:14.280 --> 40:17.680
And then you see the issue with Gaussian EKF,

40:17.680 --> 40:19.640
that one peak should be there.

40:19.640 --> 40:20.840
One peak should be there.

40:20.840 --> 40:22.440
With a Gaussian, the mean is here,

40:22.440 --> 40:25.360
which is guaranteed that you're not there.

40:25.360 --> 40:27.600
Which means that EKF should always

40:27.600 --> 40:30.680
be used for localization.

40:30.680 --> 40:34.400
So around the path, or if you have a fairly good

40:34.400 --> 40:36.240
initialization for your filter.

40:36.240 --> 40:38.660
If you have no clue, like in this case, where we put particles

40:38.660 --> 40:41.660
everywhere in the environment, then EKF is not going to work.

40:41.660 --> 40:43.940
You have to use something that is nonparametric.

40:43.940 --> 40:45.580
Or maybe something that is parametric,

40:45.580 --> 40:47.540
but uses mixtures of Gaussians and so on.

40:47.540 --> 40:49.900
Is it also fair to say that EKF is better

40:49.900 --> 40:51.940
in more sparse environments, where there's

40:51.940 --> 40:55.940
very few obstacles that would affect your probability of where

40:55.940 --> 41:00.660
you are, versus where MCL works better when you have very

41:00.660 --> 41:03.780
dense environments, where you can distinguish

41:03.800 --> 41:05.720
where you are based on the differentiation

41:05.720 --> 41:07.240
of your measurements?

41:07.240 --> 41:09.800
Well, I mean, in general, the more distinguishable landmarks

41:09.800 --> 41:13.280
you have, the better the performance of all filters is.

41:13.280 --> 41:19.800
So while it is true that EKF localization, for example,

41:19.800 --> 41:23.520
works specifically well with future-based maps,

41:23.520 --> 41:28.360
particle filters is actually more broadly applicable.

41:28.360 --> 41:30.360
The fact of having distinguishing landmarks

41:30.360 --> 41:33.640
is something that is advantageous for all filters.

41:33.640 --> 41:37.060
So there's no particular difference among them.

41:37.060 --> 41:37.560
Yeah?

41:37.560 --> 41:39.500
What do the blue lines represent?

41:39.500 --> 41:40.000
What's that?

41:40.000 --> 41:40.500
Sorry?

41:40.500 --> 41:41.980
What do the blue lines represent?

41:41.980 --> 41:43.020
Oh, measurements.

41:43.020 --> 41:47.660
So we're assuming that we have a line of sight measurement

41:47.660 --> 41:51.780
with, I think, 10 degrees of difference between the rays.

41:51.780 --> 41:53.940
So the line of the solids can stop at walls,

41:53.940 --> 41:56.220
and some go through walls?

41:56.220 --> 41:58.180
Oh, yeah, I noticed a clocking issue.

41:58.180 --> 41:58.680
Oh, OK.

42:01.740 --> 42:02.340
Cool.

42:02.340 --> 42:02.820
All right.

42:02.820 --> 42:13.800
So what we're going to talk about now is EKF SLAM.

42:13.800 --> 42:15.400
So we solved the localization problem.

42:15.400 --> 42:16.860
But in the localization problem, we

42:16.860 --> 42:18.680
assume that we know the map.

42:18.680 --> 42:20.520
What if we do not know the map?

42:20.520 --> 42:22.440
That's the problem of simultaneous localization

42:22.440 --> 42:23.440
and mapping.

42:23.440 --> 42:26.560
So what we're going to do now is to learn about, in general,

42:26.560 --> 42:28.880
what the SLAM problem entails and how

42:28.880 --> 42:32.680
to apply everything we know about parametric filters

42:32.680 --> 42:35.820
in particular EKF filters to the SLAM problem, which will

42:35.820 --> 42:38.400
lead to the EKF SLAM algorithm.

42:38.400 --> 42:40.400
On Friday, I was supposed to cover

42:40.400 --> 42:42.960
the field of non-parametric SLAM algorithms,

42:42.960 --> 42:44.360
but I'll be on travel.

42:44.360 --> 42:47.560
So we'll have a make-up lecture later on.

42:47.560 --> 42:50.400
Non-parametric filters are not covered by the homework.

42:50.400 --> 42:53.640
So it's not a problem that this make-up lecture is held either

42:53.640 --> 42:57.240
next week or in two weeks from now.

42:57.240 --> 42:59.620
Readings as usual, probabilistic robotics

42:59.620 --> 43:04.140
from Sebastian Stern, Barger than Dieter Fox, chapter 8

43:04.140 --> 43:06.380
and 10.

43:06.380 --> 43:08.660
So in a nutshell, the SLAM problem

43:08.660 --> 43:14.380
entails finding the path or the pose of a robot

43:14.380 --> 43:17.420
along with the map, given the history of the measurements

43:17.420 --> 43:19.220
from the initial time to the current time

43:19.220 --> 43:23.740
and the history of the controls from the initial time

43:23.740 --> 43:25.020
to the current time.

43:25.020 --> 43:29.500
So here, for example, I'm showing some results

43:29.500 --> 43:37.940
from my work about doing SLAM for a hopping spacecraft that

43:37.940 --> 43:43.100
is landing on an asteroid and has to map the asteroid

43:43.100 --> 43:48.500
as it's estimating its pose.

43:48.500 --> 43:50.220
So this asteroid usually is very small

43:50.220 --> 43:52.820
with very low gravitational fields.

43:52.820 --> 43:54.860
So it takes a while to get down.

43:54.860 --> 43:57.620
The reds are the observations that are made.

43:57.620 --> 44:01.860
So this is done on an asteroid mock-up down at JPL

44:01.860 --> 44:03.220
with lighting conditions that are

44:03.220 --> 44:05.780
very representative of what you have on an asteroid.

44:05.780 --> 44:08.500
So here you see that the robot is jumping.

44:08.500 --> 44:11.180
And in general, the SLAM algorithm

44:11.180 --> 44:19.860
does a pretty good job in terms of tracing the trajectory.

44:19.860 --> 44:22.080
This is not very easy for a couple of reasons.

44:22.080 --> 44:27.140
First of all, you can see that the lighting conditions are

44:27.180 --> 44:30.580
either very bright or very dark.

44:30.580 --> 44:34.820
And two, a hopping motion is discontinuous.

44:34.820 --> 44:38.340
You jump, you hit, and then you go back.

44:38.340 --> 44:40.300
But you can see that this can be done actually

44:40.300 --> 44:42.540
quite reliably in real time.

44:42.540 --> 44:44.620
So that's basically SLAM.

44:44.620 --> 44:46.460
But this is SLAM on steroids because we

44:46.460 --> 44:49.980
have additional issues in terms of picking up landmarks,

44:49.980 --> 44:53.460
very discontinuous motion, large-scale variations,

44:53.460 --> 44:54.940
and so on.

44:54.940 --> 44:58.140
But specifically, the SLAM problem

44:58.140 --> 45:03.220
entails estimating, given the history of the measurements

45:03.220 --> 45:06.220
and history of the controls, the current pose

45:06.220 --> 45:07.340
and the current map.

45:07.340 --> 45:10.220
So before the map was a conditioning variable.

45:10.220 --> 45:12.460
Now it's a variable that we want to estimate.

45:12.460 --> 45:15.100
So we move the map from the conditioning variable

45:15.100 --> 45:19.100
to the variables that we want to estimate.

45:19.100 --> 45:22.060
As it was the case for localization,

45:22.100 --> 45:25.500
solving the SLAM problem requires,

45:25.500 --> 45:29.060
as a primitive procedure, to be able to associate

45:29.060 --> 45:30.860
a measurement with a landmark.

45:30.860 --> 45:34.860
So if that correspondence is not known,

45:34.860 --> 45:36.340
which is typically the case,

45:36.340 --> 45:40.060
then we also have to estimate the correspondence variables.

45:41.260 --> 45:42.580
And in particular, there are two versions

45:42.580 --> 45:45.980
of the SLAM problem, online SLAM and full SLAM.

45:45.980 --> 45:49.060
In online SLAM, we're only trying to estimate

45:49.060 --> 45:52.420
the current pose and the map

45:52.420 --> 45:55.620
in terms of landmarks within the map.

45:55.620 --> 45:58.300
In full SLAM, we're trying to estimate

45:58.300 --> 46:02.700
the entire trajectory of the robot along with the map.

46:04.100 --> 46:08.580
Different algorithms address one or the other.

46:08.580 --> 46:13.580
Typically, the online SLAM leads to sequential algorithms

46:14.300 --> 46:17.500
like EKF SLAM, so it's computationally very tractable.

46:17.660 --> 46:20.180
Of course, in theory, having an estimate

46:20.180 --> 46:25.180
of the entire past trajectory is even more powerful.

46:25.780 --> 46:28.460
In both cases, you have the version

46:28.460 --> 46:29.820
without correspondence variable

46:29.820 --> 46:31.540
and with correspondence variable.

46:31.540 --> 46:33.660
Typically, you don't have the correspondence variables

46:33.660 --> 46:36.980
given to you, so that's a problem that you have to solve.

46:36.980 --> 46:39.020
Now, the reason why SLAM is complicated

46:39.020 --> 46:41.660
is due to two main reasons.

46:41.660 --> 46:44.740
One is that it merges optimization

46:44.780 --> 46:49.780
over continuous variables and over combinatorial variables.

46:49.780 --> 46:54.780
In particular, the positions of the landmarks,

46:55.300 --> 46:59.420
the pose of your robot is a continuous quantity.

46:59.420 --> 47:02.060
Your heading, it can be anything between zero and two pi.

47:02.060 --> 47:03.980
Your position could be anything

47:03.980 --> 47:06.180
within a given environment and so on.

47:06.180 --> 47:09.900
The correspondence of measurement to landmarks

47:09.900 --> 47:12.620
is actually a combinatorial problem.

47:12.620 --> 47:14.340
You have basically integer variables

47:14.900 --> 47:17.740
that are assigned to each measurement and landmark.

47:17.740 --> 47:18.980
So it's a very hybrid problem

47:18.980 --> 47:22.380
where some variables are continuous, other are integer.

47:23.460 --> 47:26.380
Second of all, look at this probability distribution.

47:26.380 --> 47:28.740
When we were solving the localization problem,

47:30.660 --> 47:33.700
we were finding the posteriors with respect to the pose.

47:33.700 --> 47:35.260
So the map was a conditioning variable

47:35.260 --> 47:36.820
with respect to the pose.

47:36.820 --> 47:39.700
So for example, if you have a unicycle robot

47:39.700 --> 47:41.860
with three state variables, X, Y, and theta,

47:42.420 --> 47:44.700
this, what we have is that the belief

47:44.700 --> 47:47.780
is a probability distribution over how many variables?

47:51.980 --> 47:54.820
The belief is a probability distribution over X, T

47:54.820 --> 47:56.180
for localization problems.

47:57.140 --> 47:59.540
So if X, T is three-dimensional,

47:59.540 --> 48:01.180
the belief is a probability distribution

48:01.180 --> 48:02.380
over how many variables?

48:04.300 --> 48:06.780
Three, yes, yeah, that's not a trick question.

48:07.780 --> 48:12.180
Now we have a map, basically a collection of landmarks.

48:12.180 --> 48:15.900
And typically, in the process of SLAM,

48:15.900 --> 48:19.140
you conduct many, many landmarks, even like a thousand,

48:19.140 --> 48:22.060
which means that now you have a posterior

48:22.060 --> 48:23.420
over how many variables?

48:25.700 --> 48:28.580
Thousands, right, so you went from a problem

48:28.580 --> 48:30.020
where you had a probability distribution

48:30.020 --> 48:33.060
over two or three variables to a problem

48:33.060 --> 48:34.460
and now you have a posterior distribution

48:34.500 --> 48:37.380
over 1,000, 10,000 variables.

48:37.380 --> 48:39.700
So okay, so that's not very encouraging.

48:44.220 --> 48:45.380
And there is another challenge,

48:45.380 --> 48:47.060
but it's actually also an opportunity

48:47.060 --> 48:48.740
that I'm going to discuss in a second.

48:48.740 --> 48:51.380
But before doing so, we're using our

48:52.660 --> 48:55.220
beloved graphical model for the problem.

48:55.220 --> 49:00.220
So now, in filtering, we had states, controls, measurements.

49:02.300 --> 49:03.540
We didn't have a map.

49:05.100 --> 49:08.900
In localization, we have a map and we assume we know it.

49:08.900 --> 49:13.900
Now in SLAM, the map influences our localization

49:14.380 --> 49:16.940
and process, but we don't have access to the map,

49:16.940 --> 49:19.100
so we have to estimate the map.

49:19.100 --> 49:24.100
In online SLAM, we estimate the current pose

49:25.580 --> 49:27.260
and the map.

49:27.260 --> 49:30.740
In full SLAM, we estimate the full thing and the map.

49:35.460 --> 49:40.460
That the map influences, of course,

49:41.740 --> 49:45.660
the measurements in both ways, but also their controls.

49:45.660 --> 49:48.100
For example, because of the state transition model,

49:48.100 --> 49:50.060
you cannot go through an obstacle.

49:54.420 --> 49:55.820
This is just to distinguish the relation

49:55.820 --> 49:57.420
with respect to the measurement.

49:59.340 --> 50:01.380
So now the aspect of SLAM, this is a challenge,

50:01.380 --> 50:03.260
but also an opportunity as follows.

50:03.300 --> 50:05.820
That the error with respect to the path

50:05.820 --> 50:08.780
is correlated with the error with respect to the map.

50:08.780 --> 50:09.780
What does that mean?

50:11.140 --> 50:13.380
Assume that at the beginning,

50:13.380 --> 50:14.980
there is no uncertainty about where you are.

50:14.980 --> 50:19.980
So basically, the global reference system

50:20.020 --> 50:22.900
is simply coinciding with your local reference system.

50:24.140 --> 50:26.300
You don't know the map, so you can put

50:26.300 --> 50:29.220
your initial global reference system anywhere.

50:29.220 --> 50:31.140
The challenge is that as you move,

50:31.140 --> 50:33.940
you want to keep that reference system consistent.

50:33.940 --> 50:37.100
So the uncertainty about the landmarks

50:37.100 --> 50:38.780
is only due to measurement noise.

50:39.820 --> 50:42.380
But now, as you move,

50:45.180 --> 50:47.540
you have uncertainty about where the landmark is,

50:47.540 --> 50:51.020
which means that we have uncertainty about where the map is.

50:51.020 --> 50:54.180
But we also have uncertainty about where we are.

50:54.180 --> 50:57.580
And these two uncertainties are actually related,

50:57.580 --> 51:01.940
meaning that if, so the purple ellipse

51:01.940 --> 51:03.700
is an uncertainty ellipse.

51:03.700 --> 51:06.740
So if I know that I'm here,

51:06.740 --> 51:08.940
that reduces my uncertainty about the landmark,

51:08.940 --> 51:10.900
because then I know that the landmark

51:10.900 --> 51:12.740
most likely is around here.

51:12.740 --> 51:14.260
If I know that I'm here,

51:14.260 --> 51:16.660
then that reduces my uncertainty about the landmark,

51:16.660 --> 51:19.700
because I know that the landmark most likely is here.

51:19.700 --> 51:23.180
Which means that by having more information about the map,

51:23.180 --> 51:25.620
I can better constrain information about the pose,

51:25.620 --> 51:27.180
and by having better information about the pose,

51:27.180 --> 51:29.140
I can better constrain information about the map.

51:29.140 --> 51:31.300
Which means that the two errors are related.

51:32.540 --> 51:33.380
So it's a challenge,

51:33.380 --> 51:35.540
because then to reason about the problem correctly,

51:35.540 --> 51:37.860
we have to reason about this correlation.

51:37.860 --> 51:39.300
It's an opportunity,

51:39.300 --> 51:42.820
because it means that if at some point

51:42.820 --> 51:46.500
I'm able to reduce the uncertainty about where I am,

51:46.500 --> 51:51.100
I can also immediately reduce the uncertainty about the map.

51:51.100 --> 51:53.260
And we will see that more clearly later on.

51:53.260 --> 51:59.100
Okay, as usual, what we do for ZLAM

51:59.100 --> 52:03.820
is to embed the ZLAM problem as a filtering problem.

52:03.820 --> 52:06.020
And as usual, there are two ways to approach it.

52:06.020 --> 52:08.780
Either through parametric approaches,

52:08.780 --> 52:13.100
whereby we approximate the belief

52:13.100 --> 52:15.460
according to a functional relation,

52:15.460 --> 52:17.100
like for example, a Gaussian,

52:17.100 --> 52:18.460
or a non-parametric approach.

52:18.460 --> 52:21.660
Basically, we approximate the belief

52:21.660 --> 52:23.820
through a particle filter.

52:25.300 --> 52:28.580
Now, today we're going to talk about

52:28.580 --> 52:31.020
the EKF approach to ZLAM.

52:31.020 --> 52:32.340
And we make two assumptions.

52:32.340 --> 52:35.420
So the first assumption is really the EKF assumption.

52:35.420 --> 52:39.260
We assume that the motion process noise

52:39.260 --> 52:41.900
and the measurement noise are Gaussians.

52:41.900 --> 52:44.420
And we assume, and this is also essential,

52:44.420 --> 52:47.220
that the map is future-based.

52:47.220 --> 52:49.900
For example, in terms of point landmarks.

52:52.020 --> 52:53.860
And we have two versions of the problem as usual.

52:53.860 --> 52:55.780
Either the correspondence variables are known,

52:55.780 --> 52:58.620
or the correspondence variables are not known.

52:59.580 --> 53:00.940
We will first discuss the case

53:00.940 --> 53:03.220
where the correspondence variables are known.

53:05.460 --> 53:09.740
So again, if you understand the foundation of a filtering,

53:09.740 --> 53:13.780
the EKF ZLAM algorithm is not that complicated notionally.

53:13.780 --> 53:16.220
Making it work is actually quite complicated.

53:16.220 --> 53:18.260
But notionally, it's not that complicated.

53:19.260 --> 53:20.620
Why?

53:20.620 --> 53:22.980
Because basically what we do is to,

53:22.980 --> 53:24.820
as we did for the localization problem,

53:24.820 --> 53:27.540
to reduce the problem to a filtering problem

53:27.540 --> 53:31.940
where we apply directly the EKF result.

53:31.940 --> 53:35.300
In particular, what is the trick of EKF ZLAM?

53:35.300 --> 53:37.420
Is to augment.

53:37.420 --> 53:41.460
So remember that in ZLAM we tend to estimate concurrently

53:41.460 --> 53:44.820
both the pose, xt, and the map.

53:44.820 --> 53:47.940
So the trick is to augment the state vector.

53:47.940 --> 53:51.540
And now reason in terms of a state vector, yt,

53:52.740 --> 53:57.580
that comprises the state of the robot.

53:57.580 --> 53:58.420
So yt,

54:00.180 --> 54:03.540
comprises the state of the robot, x of t.

54:03.540 --> 54:08.540
But also, all possible landmarks.

54:08.940 --> 54:10.340
We are in the situation where we assume

54:10.340 --> 54:12.660
that we know the correspondence variables.

54:12.660 --> 54:14.700
So we know how many landmarks we have.

54:14.700 --> 54:16.300
We are assuming point landmarks.

54:16.340 --> 54:18.540
So a point landmark is represented

54:18.540 --> 54:21.540
by the x and y coordinate of that landmark.

54:21.540 --> 54:24.340
So what we do is to augment the state vector

54:24.340 --> 54:29.140
with the x and y coordinates of each landmark.

54:32.260 --> 54:34.980
We're assuming that the correspondence variables are known,

54:34.980 --> 54:38.540
which implicitly means, since we know from

54:38.540 --> 54:41.100
which measurement which landmark corresponds to,

54:41.100 --> 54:43.540
that means that we know how many landmarks we have.

54:44.540 --> 54:49.020
We know where they are, but we know how many we have.

54:49.020 --> 54:53.100
In a realistic case, you don't have that information.

54:53.100 --> 54:55.420
And that's the case of EK epsilon

54:55.420 --> 54:58.420
with the unknown correspondences that we will look at later.

54:59.460 --> 55:01.580
So in that case, so if you don't know

55:01.580 --> 55:05.460
the number of landmarks, this is an augmented state vector

55:05.460 --> 55:07.780
that keeps growing through time.

55:07.780 --> 55:09.740
So it's not static, but actually grows.

55:10.740 --> 55:14.020
Okay, so the dimension of this augmented state vector

55:14.020 --> 55:16.300
is three, so for the case of a unicycle,

55:16.300 --> 55:18.620
we assume that the pose is,

55:18.620 --> 55:20.680
the configuration vector is three dimensional.

55:20.680 --> 55:22.980
If you have another robot, you might have more.

55:24.060 --> 55:26.800
And then we assume that we have n landmarks,

55:26.800 --> 55:27.980
two coordinates per landmark.

55:27.980 --> 55:31.260
So the dimension of this vector is three plus one.

55:32.920 --> 55:35.700
Having done that, so basically the idea of EK epsilon

55:35.700 --> 55:38.440
is to run something that is very similar

55:38.440 --> 55:40.720
to EKF localization with respect

55:40.720 --> 55:42.240
to the augmented state vector.

55:45.320 --> 55:47.900
And in particular, we want to,

55:50.000 --> 55:51.320
sorry, here there's a typo.

55:51.320 --> 55:52.840
There shouldn't be an M here,

55:52.840 --> 55:56.740
because M is already accounted for in Y of T.

55:56.740 --> 55:58.480
I will correct this.

55:58.480 --> 56:00.840
So we want to compute the full line posterior,

56:00.840 --> 56:03.640
so probability of YT, given the history of the measurement

56:03.640 --> 56:05.200
and the history of the controls.

56:09.120 --> 56:11.160
Okay, so as I said, now that we have a notion

56:11.160 --> 56:12.880
of augmented vector, the idea is literally

56:12.880 --> 56:14.640
to apply the EKF localization

56:14.640 --> 56:16.720
with respect to this augmented vector.

56:16.720 --> 56:20.160
As usual, in order to do so, we need a couple of things.

56:20.160 --> 56:23.120
First, to specify the transition model.

56:24.440 --> 56:27.900
We assume we know the state transition model for the robot,

56:27.900 --> 56:29.760
but what about the state transition model

56:29.760 --> 56:31.860
for the augmented vector?

56:31.860 --> 56:33.740
The assumption that we make

56:33.740 --> 56:36.400
is that the environment is static.

56:36.440 --> 56:38.940
We will briefly discuss what to do in dynamic environments.

56:38.940 --> 56:43.600
But we make the assumption that the environment is static.

56:43.600 --> 56:47.480
So what that means is that YT plus one,

56:49.600 --> 56:52.720
or actually YT, is equal to,

56:52.720 --> 56:56.480
so the first three components are equal to G,

56:58.320 --> 57:01.080
X T minus one, U T.

57:02.080 --> 57:07.080
And the other two end components are equal to YT minus one.

57:08.080 --> 57:11.720
So we assume that the environment is static.

57:11.720 --> 57:13.160
So we don't know what the landmark is,

57:13.160 --> 57:14.920
but we know that they don't move.

57:14.920 --> 57:19.760
So the coordinates that we're trying to estimate,

57:19.760 --> 57:22.240
we know that the underlying true values do not move.

57:22.240 --> 57:24.460
So that's why YT is equal to YT minus one.

57:25.340 --> 57:30.340
So this is a three by one vector.

57:32.080 --> 57:35.760
Motion is affected by noise,

57:35.760 --> 57:38.960
but we know that the landmarks do not move.

57:38.960 --> 57:40.880
So the noise is only with respect

57:40.880 --> 57:43.220
to the motion of the vehicle.

57:43.220 --> 57:46.440
So we have noise with respect to the first three variables,

57:46.440 --> 57:50.300
but we have no noise with respect to the other variables.

57:50.300 --> 57:52.580
To write everything in a compact form,

57:52.580 --> 57:54.480
we just write plus epsilon,

57:54.480 --> 57:59.480
which is also vector with the three plus two end components.

57:59.800 --> 58:01.800
But we assume that R T,

58:05.480 --> 58:08.280
so the covariance, has zero entries

58:08.280 --> 58:11.360
except for the top three by three blocks,

58:11.360 --> 58:14.440
which means that we're adding zero noise

58:14.440 --> 58:17.360
for the last two end components,

58:17.360 --> 58:18.900
meaning this has no noise,

58:20.760 --> 58:22.360
but we have noise for this one.

58:27.440 --> 58:28.640
Any question about this?

58:29.780 --> 58:31.420
So here I'm saying just something very simple.

58:31.420 --> 58:33.080
We have a documented vector.

58:33.080 --> 58:36.280
Two end components correspond to the static landmarks.

58:36.280 --> 58:38.820
So the state transition model is trivial.

58:38.820 --> 58:42.600
YT is equal to YT minus one, YT minus one, and so on.

58:42.600 --> 58:44.760
The first three that correspond to the pose of the robot,

58:44.760 --> 58:47.100
three dimensional because we are considering a unit cycle,

58:47.100 --> 58:51.540
is affected by noise, that is zero mean and Gaussian.

58:54.080 --> 58:59.080
Measurement and, as usual, this G is in general non-linear.

58:59.080 --> 59:01.000
We want to use EKF.

59:01.000 --> 59:02.920
So for all the reasons that we've already explained

59:02.920 --> 59:07.600
multiple times, we linearize the transition model

59:07.600 --> 59:10.440
with respect to the most likely state,

59:10.440 --> 59:12.240
sorry, most likely pose,

59:12.240 --> 59:14.280
in order to avoid any confusion with the momentum state,

59:14.280 --> 59:15.360
new T minus one.

59:15.360 --> 59:17.340
G, as usual, denotes the Jacobian.

59:18.760 --> 59:21.440
We have to talk about the measurement model.

59:21.440 --> 59:23.680
Measurement model is the same as last time.

59:23.680 --> 59:28.680
So we consider line of sight range and bearing sensors.

59:29.000 --> 59:34.000
So given that measurement I corresponds to landmark J,

59:37.360 --> 59:41.440
then the range, we're assuming a range of bearing sensors,

59:41.440 --> 59:43.480
as we did for EKF localization.

59:43.480 --> 59:47.880
So the range is the Euclidean distance

59:47.880 --> 59:52.880
between the current position of the robot

59:53.200 --> 59:57.400
and the position of the landmark.

59:58.760 --> 01:00:00.240
So that will be the range.

01:00:00.240 --> 01:00:05.240
So this is coordinates mu J X, mu J Y,

01:00:05.640 --> 01:00:08.180
and this is X Y, the position of the robot.

01:00:09.280 --> 01:00:11.180
So that will be the range here.

01:00:11.180 --> 01:00:14.260
And then the bearing is the,

01:00:16.520 --> 01:00:18.080
so if this is the theta,

01:00:19.600 --> 01:00:21.820
if this theta is the,

01:00:23.960 --> 01:00:25.240
this angle here, phi,

01:00:25.240 --> 01:00:30.240
which is the absolute angle minus the heading, theta,

01:00:30.640 --> 01:00:31.880
plus measurement noise.

01:00:31.880 --> 01:00:36.760
This is what we already saw for the case of localization.

01:00:36.760 --> 01:00:39.920
As usual, we linearize the measurement model.

01:00:41.240 --> 01:00:44.400
The only thing, so this is just a little bit annoyance

01:00:44.400 --> 01:00:49.080
because it's a little bit of notation.

01:00:49.080 --> 01:00:51.120
But, so this will be fine.

01:00:51.120 --> 01:00:52.440
We could just stop it here,

01:00:52.440 --> 01:00:54.020
in terms of the measurement model.

01:00:54.020 --> 01:00:57.700
Computationally, when you take the,

01:00:57.700 --> 01:00:59.440
when you do the linearization,

01:00:59.440 --> 01:01:02.620
you have to linearize the function

01:01:02.620 --> 01:01:05.060
with respect to all the variables Y T.

01:01:06.320 --> 01:01:08.060
But the measurement model,

01:01:08.060 --> 01:01:11.500
assuming that the correspondence variable are known,

01:01:11.500 --> 01:01:13.460
depend on how many variables.

01:01:13.460 --> 01:01:16.060
So Y T can be a very large vector,

01:01:16.060 --> 01:01:18.000
to what, three plus one.

01:01:18.980 --> 01:01:23.980
But the measurement model,

01:01:24.380 --> 01:01:27.020
depends on how many variables.

01:01:31.980 --> 01:01:36.660
Five, X, Y, theta, M G X, M G Y.

01:01:36.660 --> 01:01:39.500
Which means that when I take derivatives,

01:01:39.500 --> 01:01:42.820
out of the three plus two N components,

01:01:42.820 --> 01:01:44.580
only five will be known zero.

01:01:44.580 --> 01:01:47.480
So it would be silly to call MATLAB or something else

01:01:47.480 --> 01:01:49.180
to take the derivative with respect to each variable.

01:01:49.180 --> 01:01:51.900
We already know which ones won't be zero.

01:01:51.900 --> 01:01:56.440
So the idea is to compute a sub Jacobian,

01:01:56.440 --> 01:01:59.740
which is only the derivative H with respect to the variables

01:01:59.740 --> 01:02:02.860
for which we know the Jacobian is not zero.

01:02:02.860 --> 01:02:06.220
And then project that back in the full,

01:02:07.100 --> 01:02:09.620
oh, shoot, in the full Jacobian,

01:02:09.620 --> 01:02:11.860
through a projection matrix.

01:02:11.860 --> 01:02:12.700
So what it is saying,

01:02:12.700 --> 01:02:14.040
we're not going too much into the details,

01:02:14.040 --> 01:02:16.340
because it's not that insightful.

01:02:16.340 --> 01:02:19.100
So here, in order to project back in the full Jacobian,

01:02:19.100 --> 01:02:20.540
so these are the derivatives

01:02:20.580 --> 01:02:24.540
with respect to the pose of the robots,

01:02:24.540 --> 01:02:28.660
which will correspond to the top left block in the Jacobian.

01:02:28.660 --> 01:02:30.460
That's why here we have an identity.

01:02:32.100 --> 01:02:35.820
These are the derivatives with respect to the jth landmark.

01:02:35.820 --> 01:02:38.140
So it should only appear

01:02:40.100 --> 01:02:43.340
in a very specific position of the Jacobian.

01:02:43.340 --> 01:02:47.900
In particular, the position that corresponds to columns

01:02:48.740 --> 01:02:53.500
two j minus one and two j.

01:02:53.500 --> 01:02:55.060
So that's the identity here.

01:02:55.060 --> 01:02:56.600
Anything else?

01:02:56.600 --> 01:02:57.440
Is it zero?

01:02:59.720 --> 01:03:01.220
So this is just a little bit of bookkeeping.

01:03:01.220 --> 01:03:02.940
But the idea here, again,

01:03:02.940 --> 01:03:05.880
is that when we take the Jacobian,

01:03:05.880 --> 01:03:08.220
only the partial derivatives with respect to x t and j

01:03:08.220 --> 01:03:09.780
are non-zero.

01:03:09.780 --> 01:03:11.860
So instead of doing something silly,

01:03:11.860 --> 01:03:13.220
which is try to compute derivatives

01:03:13.220 --> 01:03:16.020
with respect to all components of y t,

01:03:16.020 --> 01:03:19.100
we only compute derivatives with respect to x t and mu j.

01:03:19.100 --> 01:03:21.580
And then, in order to make the matrices correspond

01:03:21.580 --> 01:03:23.540
in terms of dimensions,

01:03:23.540 --> 01:03:25.700
we use this big projection matrix.

01:03:29.360 --> 01:03:32.340
But notionally, there is just a computational trick

01:03:32.340 --> 01:03:33.780
to avoid some silly computations.

01:03:33.780 --> 01:03:35.460
But what we do is the standard thing

01:03:35.460 --> 01:03:37.960
that is to linearize our measurement model

01:03:37.960 --> 01:03:42.140
around the most likely value for the state,

01:03:42.140 --> 01:03:43.120
augmented state.

01:03:44.300 --> 01:03:45.340
Any questions here?

01:03:47.020 --> 01:03:47.860
All right.

01:03:49.260 --> 01:03:53.620
So last step is how to initialize the algorithm.

01:03:54.580 --> 01:03:57.620
So at the beginning, we have no clue

01:03:57.620 --> 01:03:59.500
what the map is and where we are.

01:03:59.500 --> 01:04:03.780
So it's natural for a SLAM algorithm

01:04:03.780 --> 01:04:05.980
to take the initial pose as the origin

01:04:05.980 --> 01:04:07.220
of the coordinate system.

01:04:08.580 --> 01:04:12.380
So basically, this is where we are.

01:04:12.380 --> 01:04:13.900
And we say that, for example,

01:04:13.900 --> 01:04:17.540
this is our position is the origin

01:04:17.540 --> 01:04:18.920
of the global coordinate system,

01:04:18.920 --> 01:04:22.820
and just to fix a generic coordinate system there.

01:04:22.820 --> 01:04:24.860
And the map that I will be constructing

01:04:24.860 --> 01:04:27.180
will be with respect to that coordinate system.

01:04:27.180 --> 01:04:29.220
And then what I want to do is to construct a map

01:04:29.220 --> 01:04:30.820
with respect to the coordinate system.

01:04:30.820 --> 01:04:35.480
And as the robot moves, keep track of the transformation

01:04:35.480 --> 01:04:38.020
between the local coordinate system and the robot,

01:04:38.020 --> 01:04:40.660
and the one that I fixed at the beginning

01:04:40.660 --> 01:04:43.380
as my global coordinate system.

01:04:44.420 --> 01:04:49.420
In particular, we assume that at the beginning,

01:04:50.320 --> 01:04:53.740
the value of the state for the robot, so the pose is zero.

01:04:55.260 --> 01:04:56.580
So we say that we are at the origin

01:04:56.580 --> 01:05:01.580
of the coordinate system that we enforce.

01:05:01.700 --> 01:05:05.540
And since we have no clue where all the other landmarks are,

01:05:05.540 --> 01:05:08.180
we set an expectation by the position

01:05:08.180 --> 01:05:09.540
for those landmarks to zero,

01:05:10.380 --> 01:05:13.980
and the corresponding uncertainty equal to infinity.

01:05:13.980 --> 01:05:17.220
Sigma is the uncertainty about the belief

01:05:17.220 --> 01:05:20.940
for the augmented state, so for both the pose of the robot

01:05:20.940 --> 01:05:23.380
and the positions of the landmarks.

01:05:23.380 --> 01:05:26.700
So we say that uncertainty with respect to the pose is zero,

01:05:26.700 --> 01:05:28.020
because we say that we're here,

01:05:28.020 --> 01:05:30.360
so the uncertainty that we're here is zero.

01:05:30.360 --> 01:05:31.700
And with respect to the landmarks,

01:05:31.700 --> 01:05:33.460
we say that uncertainty is infinity.

01:05:38.040 --> 01:05:39.300
Any questions about this?

01:05:40.540 --> 01:05:45.540
Cool, very last step, as I move around, I see a landmark.

01:05:48.940 --> 01:05:50.900
So the question is, how do I initialize

01:05:50.900 --> 01:05:53.440
the position of that landmark?

01:05:55.380 --> 01:05:59.780
Very natural thing to do is to take

01:05:59.780 --> 01:06:02.460
the most likely position for your robot

01:06:02.460 --> 01:06:06.220
as given by the expectation in the belief

01:06:06.220 --> 01:06:08.740
of the x and y position of the robot,

01:06:08.780 --> 01:06:11.900
and then initialize the landmark

01:06:11.900 --> 01:06:15.480
by using the range embedding measurements that I've seen,

01:06:15.480 --> 01:06:18.040
and using the measurement model, so specifically.

01:06:23.340 --> 01:06:28.340
I know that in expectation, I am at mu t x, mu t y,

01:06:32.740 --> 01:06:36.300
that's where I am, and I received,

01:06:36.300 --> 01:06:40.580
and I know that my heading is mu t theta.

01:06:47.380 --> 01:06:52.380
So I initialize the landmark by,

01:06:52.420 --> 01:06:56.200
and I have as a measurement, r t i phi t i.

01:06:58.840 --> 01:07:00.620
So this is my most likely expectation,

01:07:00.620 --> 01:07:05.060
so the landmark will be somewhere along the circle

01:07:05.060 --> 01:07:07.500
with radius r t i, where specifically

01:07:07.500 --> 01:07:10.780
it's given by the bearing angle phi t i.

01:07:12.820 --> 01:07:15.160
That's how I initialize the landmark.

01:07:15.160 --> 01:07:17.280
It's an approximation because basically I'm assuming

01:07:17.280 --> 01:07:20.820
that the right value of the state is the expected value,

01:07:20.820 --> 01:07:22.300
which in general is not true.

01:07:24.620 --> 01:07:25.580
Does it make sense?

01:07:28.780 --> 01:07:31.700
Cool, having said that, then the algorithm

01:07:31.700 --> 01:07:34.420
looks increasingly more convoluted,

01:07:34.420 --> 01:07:36.940
but again, I want to stress that intuition

01:07:36.940 --> 01:07:38.660
is always the same, and most of the mathematics

01:07:38.660 --> 01:07:39.560
is always the same.

01:07:40.520 --> 01:07:41.620
What do we do?

01:07:41.620 --> 01:07:45.020
We want to estimate the map and the pose,

01:07:48.500 --> 01:07:53.500
so we try to do that through the bias filtering approach.

01:07:54.220 --> 01:07:57.740
So as usual, the idea is to have a belief

01:07:57.740 --> 01:07:59.620
that captures probability distribution

01:07:59.620 --> 01:08:01.900
over the augmented state, not the state,

01:08:01.900 --> 01:08:03.140
over the augmented state.

01:08:04.140 --> 01:08:07.580
We have current control, current measurement,

01:08:07.580 --> 01:08:09.380
and we assume that we have the correspondence variables.

01:08:09.380 --> 01:08:11.620
For now, we're assuming we have that information,

01:08:11.620 --> 01:08:13.500
and we want to compute the posterior belief,

01:08:13.500 --> 01:08:15.940
accounting for the control and the measurement.

01:08:15.940 --> 01:08:20.940
So what we do as usual is first step, prediction.

01:08:21.060 --> 01:08:24.300
We use the knowledge about our motion,

01:08:24.300 --> 01:08:27.260
about our state transition equation

01:08:27.260 --> 01:08:31.140
in order to predict what time step t,

01:08:31.580 --> 01:08:33.940
the value of the augmented state factor will be.

01:08:35.660 --> 01:08:37.740
Here, there is no difference

01:08:37.740 --> 01:08:40.060
with respect to EKF localization.

01:08:40.060 --> 01:08:43.020
What we do is to apply the state transition model

01:08:43.020 --> 01:08:45.780
that I explained before, with the only difference

01:08:45.780 --> 01:08:50.780
is that the last two end components have trivial dynamics.

01:08:51.180 --> 01:08:53.180
So the value does not change.

01:08:57.980 --> 01:08:59.980
The uncertainty is, as usual,

01:08:59.980 --> 01:09:04.980
the sum of the uncertainty due to what I think I am,

01:09:05.100 --> 01:09:06.900
plus motion uncertainty.

01:09:09.340 --> 01:09:11.500
The more complicated thing happens

01:09:13.180 --> 01:09:15.420
when we want to incorporate measurements.

01:09:15.420 --> 01:09:19.580
So what we do is we take a measurement,

01:09:19.580 --> 01:09:22.180
we assume that we know the correspondence variables,

01:09:23.140 --> 01:09:27.500
so this measurement I then corresponds to landmark J.

01:09:27.500 --> 01:09:31.180
If the landmark J has never been seen,

01:09:31.180 --> 01:09:34.820
I initialize it according to the previous scheme.

01:09:34.820 --> 01:09:37.260
And once I've done that,

01:09:37.260 --> 01:09:39.780
then everything is almost identical

01:09:39.780 --> 01:09:42.540
to the EKF localization algorithm.

01:09:42.540 --> 01:09:47.540
I compute the measurement that I would have expected

01:09:48.020 --> 01:09:52.420
given that measurement I corresponds to landmark J.

01:09:53.500 --> 01:09:56.540
I compute the uncertainty in my measurement.

01:09:56.540 --> 01:10:01.540
I blend my estimate about what the state is

01:10:02.020 --> 01:10:04.100
with information from the measurements,

01:10:04.100 --> 01:10:06.860
and then I update the uncertainty.

01:10:06.860 --> 01:10:09.860
The main difference is that now this view

01:10:09.860 --> 01:10:13.260
is a three plus two n vector.

01:10:13.260 --> 01:10:16.220
And in particular, the Kalman filter is a full vector,

01:10:16.220 --> 01:10:19.100
which means that a measurement about one landmark

01:10:19.100 --> 01:10:20.900
allow us to refine the estimation

01:10:20.900 --> 01:10:25.540
with respect to all landmarks, position of all landmarks.

01:10:25.540 --> 01:10:27.180
We will see that more clearly in a second.

01:10:27.180 --> 01:10:31.700
Is that if statement basically like if all the probabilities

01:10:31.700 --> 01:10:34.540
are very low, like none of them actually match up?

01:10:34.540 --> 01:10:38.620
Well, we're in the non-correspondence case,

01:10:38.620 --> 01:10:43.620
so there is nothing probabilistic in say

01:10:44.580 --> 01:10:47.340
that the J has been seen or hasn't been seen,

01:10:47.340 --> 01:10:49.900
because it's basically this function that tells you directly.

01:10:49.900 --> 01:10:51.460
It's unrealistic though.

01:10:51.460 --> 01:10:53.500
Later on, we will do that probabilistically.

01:10:53.500 --> 01:10:54.500
That's exactly right.

01:10:55.460 --> 01:10:58.340
So exactly the same as the EKF localization.

01:10:58.340 --> 01:11:00.660
The main difference is that now we have

01:11:00.660 --> 01:11:02.380
an augmented state vector,

01:11:02.380 --> 01:11:07.380
and the dynamics for the landmark state variables

01:11:07.820 --> 01:11:08.660
is trivial.

01:11:08.660 --> 01:11:10.660
Basically, we just propagate that forward.

01:11:13.660 --> 01:11:16.740
Okay, so how this would look like in practice.

01:11:16.740 --> 01:11:18.340
So this is a robot that is moving,

01:11:18.340 --> 01:11:21.060
and these little blue dots are landmarks.

01:11:21.900 --> 01:11:23.380
Initially, I'm here.

01:11:24.220 --> 01:11:26.220
There is no uncertainty about where I am,

01:11:26.220 --> 01:11:28.980
because basically my local coordinate system

01:11:28.980 --> 01:11:31.820
by definition coincides with the global coordinate system.

01:11:33.420 --> 01:11:38.060
I see this landmark, and I have some uncertainty around it

01:11:38.060 --> 01:11:40.540
due to measurement noise.

01:11:40.540 --> 01:11:42.140
Then I start moving.

01:11:42.140 --> 01:11:44.260
The uncertainty about my pose increases

01:11:44.260 --> 01:11:46.260
because of motion uncertainty.

01:11:47.660 --> 01:11:49.420
I see another landmark,

01:11:49.420 --> 01:11:51.860
and now the uncertainty about this landmark

01:11:51.860 --> 01:11:56.860
is relatively larger because on top of measurement noise,

01:11:57.900 --> 01:11:59.900
I also have uncertainty about where I am.

01:12:01.260 --> 01:12:02.380
And then I keep going,

01:12:02.380 --> 01:12:06.260
and as I get information about additional landmarks,

01:12:06.260 --> 01:12:10.500
since I'm increasingly uncertain about where I am

01:12:10.500 --> 01:12:13.020
with respect to the initial reference system,

01:12:13.020 --> 01:12:15.740
also the uncertainty about where the landmark

01:12:15.740 --> 01:12:17.980
that I see is is also very large.

01:12:18.980 --> 01:12:23.340
But then, here, I see the first landmark

01:12:23.340 --> 01:12:24.940
that I saw at the beginning.

01:12:27.820 --> 01:12:29.460
What does that mean?

01:12:29.460 --> 01:12:32.940
That now, since I saw the first landmark

01:12:32.940 --> 01:12:34.780
that I saw at the beginning,

01:12:34.780 --> 01:12:39.460
I can constrain myself to a very specific location

01:12:39.460 --> 01:12:40.940
in this uncertainty ellipse,

01:12:43.300 --> 01:12:46.100
which immediately reduces the uncertainty

01:12:46.100 --> 01:12:47.300
about where the robot is.

01:12:48.860 --> 01:12:50.300
So I think it's very intuitive.

01:12:50.300 --> 01:12:55.300
So I start with seeing this chair,

01:12:55.380 --> 01:12:57.500
I move around, I don't see this chair,

01:12:57.500 --> 01:12:59.940
so increasingly less confident about where I am,

01:12:59.940 --> 01:13:01.740
but all of a sudden, I see this chair,

01:13:01.740 --> 01:13:04.660
I know that I'm one meter away from this chair,

01:13:04.660 --> 01:13:08.300
and that constrains the estimate about my entire past path.

01:13:09.620 --> 01:13:11.420
Since I am one meter away,

01:13:11.420 --> 01:13:14.220
it's not possible that my past trajectory

01:13:14.260 --> 01:13:18.020
led me to 50 meters away, just one step behind me.

01:13:20.340 --> 01:13:21.620
Yep?

01:13:21.620 --> 01:13:23.740
The reason that we can identify that measurement

01:13:23.740 --> 01:13:25.500
as related to the same feature

01:13:25.500 --> 01:13:27.260
is because we have all correspondence parameters?

01:13:27.260 --> 01:13:28.100
Yes.

01:13:30.980 --> 01:13:34.060
Not also how the fact that I'm certain

01:13:34.060 --> 01:13:35.580
about the pose decreases,

01:13:35.580 --> 01:13:38.460
immediately decreases the uncertainty

01:13:38.460 --> 01:13:40.580
about the position of all landmarks.

01:13:41.580 --> 01:13:42.460
Why?

01:13:42.460 --> 01:13:44.860
Because if I know that I'm here,

01:13:44.860 --> 01:13:47.020
it means that...

01:13:50.540 --> 01:13:54.940
So this landmark had this heavy distribution here

01:13:54.940 --> 01:13:57.620
because it could have been anywhere in here,

01:13:57.620 --> 01:13:58.740
so the landmark correspond,

01:13:58.740 --> 01:14:01.220
if I were here, then the landmark would be here.

01:14:01.220 --> 01:14:04.340
But since I know that I'm around here,

01:14:04.340 --> 01:14:06.260
then it's very unlikely that the landmark is here,

01:14:06.260 --> 01:14:09.020
so the uncertainty is more concentrated around here,

01:14:09.020 --> 01:14:10.420
specifically around here.

01:14:10.420 --> 01:14:14.100
So that's really the magic of the EKD epsilon algorithm,

01:14:14.100 --> 01:14:19.100
that one measurement that really gives constraints

01:14:19.220 --> 01:14:20.260
well where you are,

01:14:21.180 --> 01:14:25.700
propagates throughout the estimate of all the landmarks.

01:14:25.700 --> 01:14:27.700
How that happens?

01:14:27.700 --> 01:14:31.820
Because the Kalman filter is in general fully populated

01:14:31.820 --> 01:14:35.540
and the variance, this sigma,

01:14:35.540 --> 01:14:38.340
that is how many dimensions does this variance have?

01:14:39.220 --> 01:14:40.300
How many variables we have?

01:14:40.300 --> 01:14:42.380
Three plus two n.

01:14:42.380 --> 01:14:44.820
So it's three plus two n times three plus one.

01:14:44.820 --> 01:14:46.580
This variance is a full matrix,

01:14:46.580 --> 01:14:50.180
so also the off-diagonal elements are non-zero,

01:14:50.180 --> 01:14:52.740
which means that there is correlation among those variables.

01:14:52.740 --> 01:14:55.060
And that's exactly what causes this uncertainty

01:14:55.060 --> 01:14:58.660
to shrink simultaneously once one of them shrinks.

01:15:00.900 --> 01:15:03.060
Again, intuition should be similar.

01:15:03.060 --> 01:15:05.780
If I'm very uncertain about where I am,

01:15:06.580 --> 01:15:09.980
then the landmark that I see,

01:15:09.980 --> 01:15:12.140
I also have a lot of uncertainty about where it is.

01:15:12.140 --> 01:15:13.780
By the moment I can constrain my position,

01:15:13.780 --> 01:15:16.580
I can also immediately constrain the position of the landmark

01:15:16.580 --> 01:15:17.580
that I've just seen.

01:15:21.380 --> 01:15:24.420
Okay, there is one additional step

01:15:26.340 --> 01:15:29.660
that is what to do in the case of unknown correspondences.

01:15:29.660 --> 01:15:31.840
I will only give you a preview,

01:15:31.840 --> 01:15:34.940
and then we'll go through it in more detail next time.

01:15:34.940 --> 01:15:37.820
The preview is now that we have to add

01:15:41.020 --> 01:15:44.100
an estimation step for the correspondence variable.

01:15:44.100 --> 01:15:48.620
The way we do it is by using Malanovitz distance.

01:15:48.620 --> 01:15:50.580
So what we do is very similar

01:15:50.580 --> 01:15:53.180
to the EKF localization algorithm

01:15:53.180 --> 01:15:54.620
with unknown correspondences.

01:15:54.620 --> 01:15:56.280
Every time I see a measurement,

01:15:57.700 --> 01:16:02.100
I assume that the measurement that I've seen

01:16:02.100 --> 01:16:04.380
corresponds to a new landmark.

01:16:04.380 --> 01:16:05.820
I don't know if that is the case,

01:16:05.820 --> 01:16:09.060
but without other knowledge, I assume that that is the case.

01:16:09.060 --> 01:16:12.000
Then for all possible landmarks that I've seen,

01:16:12.000 --> 01:16:15.140
including the new one that I postulate that I've seen,

01:16:15.140 --> 01:16:19.300
I compute the corresponding most likely measurement

01:16:19.300 --> 01:16:22.180
that I should have seen if measurement i corresponds

01:16:22.180 --> 01:16:25.780
to landmark j and the corresponding measurement uncertainty.

01:16:25.780 --> 01:16:29.660
And then I compute this factor pi k

01:16:29.660 --> 01:16:33.220
that tells me how the observed measurement

01:16:33.220 --> 01:16:36.980
is close to what I think I should see

01:16:36.980 --> 01:16:39.420
if measurement i corresponds to landmark j

01:16:39.420 --> 01:16:41.220
in terms of Malanovitz distance.

01:16:42.780 --> 01:16:47.780
Then I set the pi for the new landmark

01:16:48.260 --> 01:16:49.980
that I postulate equal to alpha,

01:16:49.980 --> 01:16:52.540
some number that is a design choice.

01:16:52.540 --> 01:16:57.540
If the minimum of all these pis is indeed NT plus one,

01:16:58.960 --> 01:17:00.380
so the new landmark,

01:17:00.380 --> 01:17:02.180
it means that I've seen the new landmark.

01:17:02.180 --> 01:17:03.780
So what this is doing basically

01:17:03.780 --> 01:17:06.100
is to create a new landmark

01:17:06.100 --> 01:17:08.700
if the Malanovitz distance to all existing landmarks

01:17:08.700 --> 01:17:09.900
exceeds the value alpha,

01:17:12.140 --> 01:17:14.500
which seems reasonable.

01:17:14.500 --> 01:17:16.900
Once you have the assignment, everything else is the same.

01:17:21.220 --> 01:17:24.660
Cool, well, that's it for EK epsilon.

01:17:24.660 --> 01:17:27.300
I will just give you a couple more words

01:17:27.300 --> 01:17:30.120
for EK epsilon with unknown correspondences next week

01:17:30.120 --> 01:17:30.960
when I'm back.

01:17:32.780 --> 01:17:34.900
But where will you take it from here?

01:17:34.900 --> 01:17:36.180
We are not going to do it in this class,

01:17:36.180 --> 01:17:38.220
but in a more advanced class.

01:17:38.220 --> 01:17:39.700
Well, how to make it robust?

01:17:40.820 --> 01:17:44.540
Of course, oftentimes you generate,

01:17:44.540 --> 01:17:46.340
especially in the case of unknown correspondences,

01:17:46.340 --> 01:17:47.820
spurious landmarks.

01:17:47.820 --> 01:17:49.060
You see that the landmark is new,

01:17:49.060 --> 01:17:50.800
but actually it's not really new.

01:17:50.800 --> 01:17:52.420
So in order to robustify it,

01:17:52.420 --> 01:17:56.100
in general you need to add some try rejection schemes.

01:17:56.100 --> 01:17:58.580
And also, so that's for the feature itself.

01:17:58.580 --> 01:17:59.960
For the computer vision part,

01:17:59.960 --> 01:18:01.600
so when you pick up the landmarks,

01:18:01.960 --> 01:18:04.480
you try to pick those that really are distinctive.

01:18:04.480 --> 01:18:07.140
So that's really the problem on feature detection.

01:18:07.140 --> 01:18:09.820
Detecting good landmarks is really important.

01:18:09.820 --> 01:18:12.080
So that's why we spent also some time on that.

